{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Lift the heads of all the congregation of the children of Israel.</b> The elevation of Israel is only through fulfillment of the Torah, and therefore Scripture seized the expression שאו, which is an expression embracing two opposite things: one is lifting of the head, and the other is removal of the head. And thus they said in the Midrash: שאו את ראש — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I have cherished no nation more than you; therefore I have given you 'suspended head,' just as I have a 'suspended head' over all who come into the world, as it says (I Chronicles 29:11): לך ה' הממלכה והמתנשא לכל לראש, to fulfill what is said (Psalms 148:14): וירם קרן לעמו; and so it says (Deuteronomy 28:1): ונתנך ה' אלהיך עליון על כל גויי הארץ. The removal of the head — in the manner of (Genesis 40:19): ישא פרעה את ראשך מעליך, like one who says to the executioner: ארים רישיה דפלן ('I will lift the head of so-and-so' — i.e., for execution). And therefore it said שאו and did not say גדל ('magnify') or רומם ('exalt') — that is, if they merit, it is an expression of elevation; if not, their elevation will be removed." "<b>By their families, by their fathers' houses,</b> because the mother's family is not called a family." "<b>By the number of names.</b> It did not say 'by the number of their names,' as it says (Numbers 3:40): ושא את מספר שמותם. It may be explained that the census of Israel in receiving the Torah at Sinai corresponded to the census of the camps of the angels that were revealed there at the holy assembly, and the number in the camps of Israel in four banners was like the number of the angels in the four camps of the Shekhinah. And therefore it said במספר שמות — that is, by the number of the names of the angels — for Israel at that holy assembly were bodily angels corresponding to the number of the ministering angels on high. And this is what it said: תפקדו אותם לצבאותם — that is, corresponding to the hosts of the Names that He mentioned, for Israel are hosts below corresponding to the hosts above; and Scripture says (Exodus 7:4): והוצאתי את צבאותי את עמי בני ישראל." "<b>And you should</b> reflect on what Scripture says (Exodus 12:41): יצאו כל צבאות ה' מארץ מצרים — and it should have said 'all the hosts of Israel,' but it said 'all the hosts of YHWH' to include in the exile both those above and those below. And even more than this they said: the Shekhinah was exiled with them, and there is no doubt that once the Shekhinah was with them in exile, the hosts of the four camps of the Shekhinah were with them; and from this it says regarding their redemption that they went out with them. And what will strengthen this for you, in my opinion, is what Scripture says: ויהיו כל פקודי בני ישראל לבית אבותם — the intent is regarding the hosts below; and it returned a second time: ויהיו כל הפקודים — the intent is regarding the hosts above, for they were equal in number; and this is why it did not mention 'Israel' here." "<b>And reflect</b> further, after the arrangement of the journeys, and you will find this very expression repeated: (Numbers 2:32) אלה פקודי בני ישראל לבית אבותם — these are the hosts below; כל פקודי המחנה לצבאותם — these are the hosts above, which are the four camps of the Shekhinah."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:45.344095067Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}